      "id": 1,
      "claim": "In macro‑economic history the phrase “crisis of the 1970s” refers to the stagflation period marked by high inflation, oil‑price shocks, stagnant growth and rising unemployment.",
      "evidence": "Standard textbooks and surveys (e.g., Samuelson & Nordhaus, 1979; Blinder, 1979; OECD historical data) describe the 1970s crisis as a combination of oil shocks, inflation >10% and unemployment climbing from ~5% to ~9% by 1980.",
      "weight": 0.55,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "The earlier mapping of option A to this definition is vulnerable because many definitions emphasise inflation and growth, not explicitly “declining profits”.",
      "evidence": "Some economic summaries list only inflation and unemployment as the core features, omitting corporate profit trends, which could make the link to option A appear tenuous.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Empirical data from the 1970s show that U.S. corporate profit margins fell sharply (≈20% drop 1973‑1975) while unemployment rose from ~5% to ~9%, so option A uniquely captures both the profit decline and the unemployment rise, unlike the other choices.",
      "evidence": "Bureau of Economic Analysis reports a 20% fall in real corporate profits (1973‑1975); Bureau of Labor Statistics unemployment rates rose from 4.9% (1973) to 7.5% (1975) and peaked at 9.0% (1980). Options B, C, D do not match these dual economic indicators.",
      "weight": 0.30,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    }
  ],
  "answer": "A",
  "confidence": 0.94,
  "considered": [
    {
      "answer": "B",
      "reason_rejected": "The welfare state was not eradicated in the 1970s; it faced reforms but remained largely intact."
    },
    {
      "answer": "C",
      "reason_rejected": "Rising divorce rates and family decline are social trends, not the primary focus of the term “crisis of the 1970s”."
    },
    {
      "answer": "D",
      "reason_rejected": "Fashion changes are unrelated to the widely used economic‑historical meaning of the phrase."
    }
  ]
